\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Yes, quite a bit actually. NumPy is the invisible layer underneath almost everything librosa does \'97 every audio array, every feature matrix, every STFT output is a NumPy array. You won't always call NumPy directly, but you need to understand its concepts to make sense of what librosa returns and to do anything non-trivial with the results.\
Here's what comes up exercise by exercise:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Exercises 1\'962 (load, waveform)
\f0\b0  \'97 light NumPy. You need array shape (
\f2\fs26 array.shape
\f0\fs24 ), 
\f2\fs26 dtype
\f0\fs24 , and the idea that the audio signal is just a 1D float array. 
\f2\fs26 len(y)
\f0\fs24  and 
\f2\fs26 y.shape[0]
\f0\fs24  are the same thing, and knowing why matters.\

\f1\b Exercise 3\'964 (STFT, spectrogram)
\f0\b0  \'97 this is where NumPy becomes essential. 
\f2\fs26 np.abs()
\f0\fs24  extracts magnitude from complex numbers. You need to understand what a 2D array's shape 
\f2\fs26 (frequencies, frames)
\f0\fs24  means and how to index it. The 
\f2\fs26 n_fft
\f0\fs24  and 
\f2\fs26 hop_length
\f0\fs24  parameters directly control the output shape, and understanding why requires knowing how array dimensions work.\

\f1\b Exercise 5 (phase extraction)
\f0\b0  \'97 the most NumPy-heavy exercise in the fundamentals tier. You need 
\f2\fs26 np.angle()
\f0\fs24  to extract phase, 
\f2\fs26 np.exp()
\f0\fs24  and the imaginary unit 
\f2\fs26 1j
\f0\fs24  to reconstruct the complex matrix, and 
\f2\fs26 np.allclose()
\f0\fs24  to verify the result. You also need to understand complex number arrays as a dtype (
\f2\fs26 complex64
\f0\fs24 ), not just as a math concept.\

\f1\b Exercises 6 (ISTFT, filtering)
\f0\b0  \'97 slicing. Zeroing out the top half of frequency bins means 
\f2\fs26 S[:n_fft//2, :] = 0
\f0\fs24 , which requires understanding row/column indexing and slice notation. This is pure NumPy array manipulation.\

\f1\b Exercises 7\'969 (chroma, MFCCs, deltas)
\f0\b0  \'97 you need 
\f2\fs26 axis=
\f0\fs24  parameter awareness. When you compute per-coefficient mean and standard deviation for normalization, you do 
\f2\fs26 np.mean(mfcc, axis=1, keepdims=True)
\f0\fs24  \'97 and getting 
\f2\fs26 axis=
\f0\fs24  wrong silently produces a wrong result with no error. 
\f2\fs26 keepdims=True
\f0\fs24  matters for broadcasting to work correctly. You also need basic broadcasting intuition: subtracting a 
\f2\fs26 (13, 1)
\f0\fs24  array from a 
\f2\fs26 (13, T)
\f0\fs24  array works because of broadcasting rules.\

\f1\b Exercise 10 (manual vector comparison)
\f0\b0  \'97 explicit NumPy math. 
\f2\fs26 np.dot(a, b)
\f0\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 np.linalg.norm(v)
\f0\fs24 , and knowing how to slice a single column from a matrix with 
\f2\fs26 [:, frame_index]
\f0\fs24 . The cosine similarity formula requires you to combine all three correctly.\

\f1\b Exercises 11\'9613 (similarity matrices)
\f0\b0  \'97 you mainly need to interpret the matrix shapes and index into them. 
\f2\fs26 np.argmax()
\f0\fs24  and 
\f2\fs26 np.argsort()
\f0\fs24  become useful for finding the most similar frames. You also need to be comfortable with the fact that 
\f2\fs26 cross_similarity()
\f0\fs24  returns a 2D float array and that you can treat it like any other matrix.\

\f1\b Exercise 14\'9615 (DTW, full pipeline)
\f0\b0  \'97 
\f2\fs26 np.flipud()
\f0\fs24  comes up because DTW returns a cost matrix that needs to be flipped for standard visualization orientation. You need to be comfortable stacking arrays with 
\f2\fs26 np.vstack()
\f0\fs24  or 
\f2\fs26 np.concatenate()
\f0\fs24  for combining feature matrices, and 
\f2\fs26 np.nan
\f0\fs24  / 
\f2\fs26 np.nanmean()
\f0\fs24  for handling missing values when aligning tracks of different lengths.\
The concepts worth studying, roughly in order of how much they'll unblock you:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 array.shape
\f0\fs24  and 
\f2\fs26 ndim
\f0\fs24  \'97 understanding what 
\f2\fs26 (1025, 216)
\f0\fs24  means for an STFT output is prerequisite to everything else. 
\f2\fs26 dtype
\f0\fs24  \'97 knowing the difference between 
\f2\fs26 float32
\f0\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 float64
\f0\fs24 , and 
\f2\fs26 complex64
\f0\fs24 . Array indexing and slicing \'97 rows, columns, ranges, the 
\f2\fs26 [:, i]
\f0\fs24  column-slice pattern. 
\f2\fs26 axis=
\f0\fs24  parameter \'97 which direction an operation collapses. Broadcasting \'97 why 
\f2\fs26 (13, 200) - (13, 1)
\f0\fs24  works without a loop. 
\f2\fs26 np.abs()
\f0\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 np.angle()
\f0\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 np.exp()
\f0\fs24  \'97 the complex number toolkit. 
\f2\fs26 np.dot()
\f0\fs24  and 
\f2\fs26 np.linalg.norm()
\f0\fs24  \'97 the linear algebra basics. 
\f2\fs26 np.allclose()
\f0\fs24  \'97 for verifying numerical equality (exact equality fails with floats). 
\f2\fs26 np.mean()
\f0\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 np.std()
\f0\fs24  with 
\f2\fs26 axis=
\f0\fs24  and 
\f2\fs26 keepdims=True
\f0\fs24  \'97 the normalization toolkit.\
You don't need to know 
\f2\fs26 np.fft
\f0\fs24 , matrix decomposition, or anything from 
\f2\fs26 np.linalg
\f0\fs24  beyond 
\f2\fs26 norm
\f0\fs24 . The librosa functions handle the heavy math internally. What you need is fluency with the array as a data structure \'97 shapes, indexing, and the handful of mathematical operations that come up repeatedly.\
}